      ":question/id": "9b6d1f21-07f3-507b-b298-961146e6898a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{9}\\right)\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{18}\\right)-1}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{18}\\right)+\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{18}\\right)}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Write $s=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{18}\\right)$ and $c=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{18}\\right)$. The **double-angle formula** gives $\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{9}\\right)=2sc$.\n\n Also, $\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{18}\\right)=\\frac{s}{c}$.\n\nRewrite the quotient ratio and use $s^2+c^2=1$ to obtain a difference of squares. Factor before cancelling.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2sc\\frac{s}{c}-1}{s+c} &=\\frac{2s^2-1}{s+c} \\\\ &=\\frac{s^2-c^2}{s+c} \\\\ &=\\frac{(s-c)(s+c)}{s+c} \\\\ &=s-c \\\\ &=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{18}\\right)-\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{18}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll cancellations use nonzero factors in the original expression.",
